WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:08.023
What happens when a rope grows stronger with voltage, or a modulator changes the

2
00:00:08.023 --> 00:00:09.023
ropes themselves?

3
00:00:09.833 --> 00:00:12.493
A real neuron is not one compact patch.

4
00:00:13.313 --> 00:00:17.133
Each patch of dendrite has its own ropes, and passes current to its neighbours.

5
00:00:18.267 --> 00:00:20.547
So the patches set each other's voltages.

6
00:00:21.407 --> 00:00:25.627
The length constant and the capacitance shrink and smooth what reaches the soma.

7
00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:32.310
Polsky, Mel and Schiller stimulated two sites on thin dendrites of rat neocortical

8
00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:33.330
pyramidal neurons.

9
00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:36.090
First each alone, then both together.

10
00:00:37.133 --> 00:00:39.173
Picture their comparison as a graph.

11
00:00:40.153 --> 00:00:43.913
Along the bottom, the response expected if the two inputs simply added.

12
00:00:44.933 --> 00:00:47.473
Up the side, the response actually measured.

13
00:00:48.313 --> 00:00:51.393
A point on the 45 degree line means linear addition.

14
00:00:52.483 --> 00:00:55.843
Inputs far apart, or on different branches, fell on the line.

15
00:00:56.703 --> 00:00:57.703
They summed linearly.

16
00:00:58.667 --> 00:01:01.727
Nearby inputs on one thin branch summed sigmoidally.

17
00:01:02.247 --> 00:01:05.707
The response rose slowly, then steeply, then levelled off.

18
00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:09.020
So location set the combination rule.

19
00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:14.180
The result supports the idea that thin branches act as separate integrating

20
00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:15.180
subunits.

21
00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:19.710
The steep phase is generally attributed to local regenerative events.

22
00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:22.870
NMDA receptors are thought to take part.

23
00:01:23.833 --> 00:01:29.793
Once glutamate and a co-agonist have bound, an NMDA receptor is a rope that thickens

24
00:01:29.793 --> 00:01:31.033
as the marker rises.

25
00:01:32.053 --> 00:01:35.733
Depolarization pushes out the magnesium ion that blocks it.

26
00:01:36.617 --> 00:01:41.077
Over the right voltage range, it thickens faster than its driving force shrinks.

27
00:01:41.937 --> 00:01:42.937
Then its pull grows.

28
00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:47.730
Now modulators. A fast synapse adds a rope briefly.

29
00:01:48.650 --> 00:01:51.850
A modulator can instead change the ropes the cell already has.

30
00:01:52.833 --> 00:01:59.513
In sympathetic neurons, Gq-coupled M1 muscarinic receptors close KCNQ potassium

31
00:01:59.513 --> 00:02:02.113
channels. These carry the M-current.

32
00:02:03.133 --> 00:02:08.633
In cells expressing these channels, depleting the membrane lipid PIP2 alone was

33
00:02:08.633 --> 00:02:09.973
enough to suppress the current.

34
00:02:10.733 --> 00:02:12.993
So the lipid itself can regulate the channel.

35
00:02:13.967 --> 00:02:16.827
Closing these channels removes a potassium rope.

36
00:02:17.647 --> 00:02:20.067
What happens to the response to the same current step?

37
00:02:26.017 --> 00:02:31.197
In a passive approximation, closing a potassium conductance raises the resting

38
00:02:31.197 --> 00:02:33.617
voltage a little and shrinks the bottom line.

39
00:02:34.477 --> 00:02:39.377
The same sustained current step then gives a larger steady change, and takes longer

40
00:02:39.377 --> 00:02:41.017
to reach the same fraction of it.

41
00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:44.310
Closing them increases excitability.

42
00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:47.290
The neuron fires more to the same input.

43
00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:51.530
Dopamine on D1 receptors is the harder case.

44
00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:56.230
In mouse striatal neurons, firing to the same injected current rose.

45
00:02:57.250 --> 00:02:59.430
It rose within hundreds of milliseconds.

46
00:03:00.490 --> 00:03:04.250
After one bout of stimulation, it stayed raised for at least 10 minutes.

47
00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:10.520
With synaptic receptors blocked and drift controlled, the cell's own conductances or

48
00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:12.180
active currents must have changed.

49
00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:14.700
A spike count cannot say which channel.

50
00:03:15.733 --> 00:03:18.173
Branches can make a rope grow with voltage.

51
00:03:19.173 --> 00:03:20.833
Modulators change the ropes themselves.

52
00:03:21.833 --> 00:03:24.113
The rule tells you what kind of change to look for.
